BERM is covered by two licences, because code and documentation want
different things from a licence.

  Code                    MIT            see LICENSE-MIT.txt
  Documentation,          CC BY-SA 4.0   see LICENSE-CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt
  hardware designs,
  and written material

MIT on the code is deliberate. Creative Commons themselves advise against
using CC licences for software: they say nothing useful about linking,
derivative works, or patents, and that ambiguity is enough to make a
developer walk away. If you want to build something on BERM Bridge —
commercial, open, a game, a research tool, anything — MIT means you can,
without asking.

Everything in bridge/ is MIT. The README and any docs are CC BY-SA 4.0.
